.TH CHTTP_REQUEST_SET_HEADER 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_request_set_header \- set a request header, or replace one
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttp_request_set_header(chttp_request_t *req,
                                       const char *name,
                                       const char *value);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function adds the named header to the request, or replaces it.
The request stores every header name in lowercase, folding only the
ASCII letters A to Z whatever the process's locale, so every later
lookup ignores case. If a header with the same name (compared without
regard to case) is already there, the new value overwrites the old
one.

The header map is created on the first call.

name must be a non-empty token made only of the tchar bytes of RFC 7230
SS3.2.6: each byte must be an ASCII letter, an ASCII digit or one of
"!#$%&'*+-.^_`|~". A name of zero length has no valid form on the
wire. A name with any other byte in it, such as a space or a
literal ':', can't inject a CRLF, but it does put a line with a broken
structure on the wire, which a strict server or proxy further down
can misread.

value must hold only visible bytes, spaces and horizontal tabs (the
field-value rule of RFC 9110 section 5.5), so any other byte below
0x20, and 0x7F, is refused. Bytes of 0x80 and above are accepted. The
serializer writes "name: value\er\en" onto the wire without escaping
anything, and a caller may well reflect untrusted data into a header,
such as a token it forwards or a header from a proxy. A CR or an LF in
value would let that caller inject any number of extra header lines,
or split the request in two (HTTP request splitting), and any other
control byte makes a message that a recipient may refuse or read
differently. So for such a name or value the function returns
ccol_invalid_args and stores nothing. The client repeats the same check
when it writes a request, which means a header map that a caller builds
directly, such as the map of chttp_run_query, also fails the request
with ccol_invalid_args, and so does a body.content_type with such a
byte.

The function also rejects the name "Transfer-Encoding" (compared
without regard to case). chttpclient never applies a transfer coding to
a request body; it always sends a body whole and frames it with
Content-Length. If that name were accepted, the automatic
Content-Length of chttpclient_do would put a Content-Length header next
to the Transfer-Encoding header on the wire while the body carries no
transfer coding. That framing is ambiguous, and this library's own
request and response parser rejects it in any message it parses (RFC
7230 SS3.3.3).

The function DOES accept a "Content-Length" header, because it has no
body length to check the value against, and a check here would also
need a copy of the request's method and redirect context, which only
come later. The client validates the header later, when the request
reaches the wire. On a request that carries a body (method POST, PUT or
PATCH), chttpclient_do and every other function that runs a request
reject a bad Content-Length from the caller. A valid value is a plain
unsigned string of decimal digits, with no leading '+' or '-' and no
whitespace in it, and it must match the body that the client sends
exactly. The reason is the same as for rejecting Transfer-Encoding:
the declared framing and the wire must not disagree. See
chttpclient_do(3).

A Connection header whose value lists the "close" option, in any ASCII
case and anywhere in its comma-separated list, makes the request the
last one on its connection. The client closes the connection after the
response and never reuses it, whatever the response says (RFC 9112
section 9.6).

An Idempotency-Key or X-Idempotency-Key header (in any ASCII case)
marks the request as safe to send again. When a reused connection
accepts the whole request and then dies without a response, the client
sends it again on a new connection, as it does for a GET; see
chttpclient_do(3).

A header that you set here follows every redirect, with two
exceptions. A hop whose scheme, host or port differs from the original
request carries none of the Authorization, Cookie, Cookie2 or
WWW-Authenticate headers you set. A hop whose host differs carries no
Host header of yours, and the client writes its own instead. Each hop
is compared with the original request, so a hop back to the original
origin carries them again. Hosts are compared without regard to case.
See chttpclient_do(3).

The client writes a default value for Host, Accept, User-Agent,
Content-Length, Content-Type, Authorization and Expect when it needs
one. If you set a header with one of those names here, the client
writes no default and sends your value exactly as you gave it, Host
included.

Only POST, PUT and PATCH have a body. For every other method the client
does not send a Content-Length, Content-Type or Expect header that you
set, because there is no body for it to describe. The same holds on a
hop that a 301, 302 or 303 redirect turned into a GET.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
req     The request to change. It must not be NULL.
name    The header name, for example "Authorization". It must not be
        NULL and must not be empty. It must hold only tchar bytes of
        RFC 7230. It must not be "Transfer-Encoding".
value   The header value. It must not be NULL. It must not hold a
        byte below 0x20 other than a horizontal tab, or 0x7F.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success            The request has the header.
ccol_invalid_args       req, name or value is NULL; or name is empty,
                        or holds a byte outside the tchar set of RFC
                        7230; or value holds a byte below 0x20 other
                        than a horizontal tab (CR and LF among them),
                        or 0x7F; or name is "Transfer-Encoding".
ccol_not_enough_memory  The header map could not be allocated.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttp_request_get_header (3)
